# PerfPilot Technology Reference Policy

## Required reference workflow

Before non-trivial technology-specific implementation:
1. Identify the affected component and inspect its existing implementation.
2. Read relevant repository documentation, dependency declarations, lockfiles,
   Dockerfiles, runtime configuration, and existing tests.
3. If present, consult docs/engineering/technology-stack.md and
   docs/engineering/development-workflow.md. These are proposed shared-reference
   locations, not files whose existence may be assumed. If absent, use existing
   references and propose creating them; do not invent their contents.
4. Consult authoritative documentation relevant to the proposed change, matched
   to the approved protocol and installed/resolved package versions.
5. Record consequential URLs, versions, assumptions, and verification in the plan.

For a documentation-only change, verify the claims being edited against relevant
implementation and official sources; do not research unrelated technologies.

## Evidence and conflicts

- Approved project policy defines intended architecture and supported versions.
- Dependency constraints define allowed ranges, not exact installed versions.
- Lockfiles, environment/package output, and image configuration provide separate
  evidence about resolved packages and runtimes. State what was actually checked.
- Report discrepancies before implementation; do not silently change dependencies,
  requirements, lockfiles, runtime versions, or public contracts.
- Normative protocol specifications govern wire behavior. Version-matched library
  documentation governs library APIs. Framework examples and AI/search summaries
  do not override the normative specification.
- Do not assume a /latest/ page describes the project's approved release.
- If a source is unavailable or a key version is unknown, disclose the gap and
  ask when it blocks a grounded change. Do not claim to have consulted a URL
  merely because it appears in this file.
- Use configured research tools under the project's disclosure/security rules.

## Recorded baseline: verify against the working branch

These values come from the user's supplied project information. They are not
an automatically refreshed inventory. Do not change pins to make them match.

- Python runtime target: 3.12.4 for the identified Python backend/MCP components.
- AG2 installed: 0.13.3; declared constraint: ag2[ag-ui]>=0.13.3,<0.14.
- ag-ui-protocol installed: 0.1.19. No separate approved pin has been supplied.
- FastMCP declared constraint: >=3.4.2,<4. User reports a working 3.4.2 baseline;
  verify the affected environment rather than treating the range as an exact pin.
- No A2A SDK package is installed; inspect the existing server implementation.
- Frontend runtime supplied by user: Node.js 25.2.1. Exact CopilotKit/React package
  versions remain pending; inspect package manifests and lockfiles or ask.
- Each approved PerfPilot component is containerized; preserve the established
  A2A, AG-UI, MCP, and frontend service boundaries.

## A2A

- Approved protocol target: A2A v1. Confirm the exact release and binding needed
  for the affected work rather than inferring support from route names.
- Official specification entry point:
  https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/
- Identify a release-specific reference where available and record the release
  consulted. Do not silently follow a future change to /latest/.
- Verify affected message schemas, method names, errors, task states, streaming,
  discovery, and authentication requirements against the applicable specification.
- Do not assume an A2A SDK or SDK classes exist, or add an SDK without approval.
- Do not infer A2A compliance from AG2 or AG-UI versions, requirements comments,
  or a successful smoke test alone. Add/propose focused contract coverage.

## AG2 and AG-UI

- Follow the installed AG2 release and existing orchestrator/specialist design.
- Treat AG2 and ag-ui-protocol as distinct packages with distinct version evidence.
- Verify relevant agent lifecycle, tool execution, event ordering, streaming,
  cancellation, and error behavior against version-appropriate official sources.
- Preserve the backend/frontend integration contract. Do not invent events or
  mix A2A and AG-UI protocol concepts.

## CopilotKit and React

- Verify the component's package.json and lockfile before selecting APIs.
- Match CopilotKit/React integration guidance to resolved frontend packages and
  the backend's supported contract. Do not infer package APIs from Node version.
- No frontend test framework is established in the supplied project context.
  Inspect current tooling before proposing a minimal setup; do not install one
  or assume all frontend verification can be skipped.
- Do not import Python logging code into frontend code. Propose frontend logging
  separately if needed, preserving sensitive-data restrictions.

## FastMCP

- Target the approved version-3 implementation and >=3.4.2,<4 dependency range.
- Consult version-3 documentation or matching source/release tags; do not use
  current-major examples as proof that an API exists in the installed version.
- Preserve established gateway, service, namespace, transport, and tool contracts.
- Future-ready means isolating integration boundaries and recording verified
  migration risks. It does not authorize v4 APIs, speculative compatibility
  shims, dependency upgrades, or a migration.

## Docker and deployment

- Inspect the affected Dockerfile, Compose configuration, environment references,
  build context, startup command, health checks, and documented ports.
- Preserve approved per-component boundaries and declared runtime versions.
- Keep container secrets out of source, build arguments, image layers, and logs.
- Do not claim an image builds or a container is healthy without actual checks.
- Do not build, start, stop, or modify shared services without appropriate approval.

## Research traceability

- Keep useful source URLs, exact releases consulted, and unresolved questions in
  approved plans or shared engineering documentation as appropriate.
- Source comments/docstrings must remain self-contained and free of internal
  work-item or private planning references.
- Rules guide the agent; do not describe their presence as proof of CI enforcement
  or protocol compliance.
